Methods of Thread Class

Thread Lifecycle Methods
1) start() – Starts a new thread and calls the run() method.
2) run() – Defines the code that runs in the thread.
3) stop() (Deprecated) – Stops the thread forcefully (not recommended).
4) suspend() (Deprecated) – Suspends the thread (not recommended).
5) resume() (Deprecated) – Resumes a suspended thread (not recommended).
6) destroy() (Deprecated) – Stops the thread (not recommended).


Thread Control Methods
1) sleep(long millis) – Puts the thread to sleep for a specified time.
2) join() – Waits for the thread to finish execution.
3) join(long millis) – Waits for the thread to finish execution for a given time.
4) yield() – Suggests the thread scheduler to allow other threads to execute.
5) interrupt() – Interrupts a sleeping or waiting thread.
6) isInterrupted() – Checks if the thread has been interrupted.
7) interrupted() – Checks if the current thread has been interrupted.


Thread Priority & Naming
1) setName(String name) – Assigns a name to the thread.
2) getName() – Returns the thread's name.
3) setPriority(int priority) – Sets thread priority (MIN_PRIORITY=1, NORM_PRIORITY=5, MAX_PRIORITY=10).
4) getPriority() – Gets the thread’s priority.
5) setDaemon(boolean on) – Marks the thread as a daemon thread.
6) isDaemon() – Checks if the thread is a daemon thread.


Thread Status & Information
1) isAlive() – Checks if the thread is still running.
2) activeCount() (Static Method) – Returns the number of active threads.
3) currentThread() (Static Method) – Returns a reference to the currently executing thread.
4) holdsLock(Object obj) (Static Method) – Checks if the current thread holds the lock on the given object.
5) dumpStack() (Static Method) – Prints the stack trace of the current thread.
6) getState() – Returns the current state of the thread (NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, etc.).



